            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I want to use `i` as the prefix for `inside` commands in `operator-pending` mode, but use `h` in `visual` mode because I use `i` to move right.\n\nSorry, I don't think this aligns well with overall 'mini.ai' (and built-in textobjects even) design. Using the same keys for textobject in both Visual and Operator-pending mode is a usual case here.\n\n> This is a really niche use-case (like it might just be me) so if there is another work-around I'm happy to go for that instead.\n\nYeah, I am afraid so. The best solution I can suggest is to more or less follow in the footsteps of [how adjusting built-in `an` / `in`](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/a59a9b7fb0a42cbcf022938ee5f0724320b66f63/doc/mini-ai.txt#L574-L587) is suggested to be handled. I.e. let 'mini.ai' create its mappings, but then adjust them manually by copy necessary and delete conflicting ones. If I understand correctly the use case, it might be something like this:\n\n```lua\nrequire('mini.ai').setup()\n\nlocal move_keymap = function(mode, from_lhs, to_lhs)\n  local keymap = vim.fn.maparg(from_lhs, mode, false, true)\n  local rhs = keymap.callback or keymap.rhs\n  -- Note the use of `expr`, it is important\n  vim.keymap.set(mode, to_lhs, rhs, { desc = keymap.desc, expr = keymap.expr == 1 })\n  vim.keymap.del(mode, from_lhs)\nend\nmove_keymap('x', 'i', 'h')\nmove_keymap('x', 'in', 'hn')\nmove_keymap('x', 'il', 'hl')\n```",
